</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>State the expected-count formula under independence.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>State the Pearson chi-square statistic.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Define risk difference.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Define relative risk.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Define odds.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Define an odds ratio.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Define the logit function.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>State the simple logistic regression model.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>A table has row total <m>90</m>, column total <m>80</m>, and grand total
<m>240</m>. Compute the expected count under independence.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>For the table</p>

<p>
<md>\begin{array}{c|cc}
&amp;
Y=1
&amp;
Y=0
\\
\hline
X=1
&amp;
30
&amp;
20
\\
X=0
&amp;
15
&amp;
35
\end{array}</md>
</p>

<p>compute the event risk in each group.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Compute the risk difference for the previous table.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Compute the relative risk for the previous table.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Compute the odds ratio for the previous table.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>p=0.75,</md>
</p>

<p>compute the odds.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>If the odds are <m>4</m>, compute the corresponding probability.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>If a logistic coefficient is</p>

<p>
<md>\beta_1=0.7,</md>
</p>

<p>write the corresponding odds ratio.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>For</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{logit}(p)
=
-1+0.5x,</md>
</p>

<p>compute <m>p</m> when <m>x=2</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>A paired binary table has</p>

<p>
<md>b=12,
\qquad
c=4.</md>
</p>

<p>Compute the large-sample McNemar statistic.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>For an <m>r\times c</m> table with <m>r=4</m> and <m>c=3</m>, find the
chi-square degrees of freedom.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Suppose <m>X^2=18</m>, <m>n=200</m>, <m>r=3</m>, and <m>c=4</m>. Compute
Cramér's <m>V</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Derive the expected-count formula under independence.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Show that the Pearson chi-square statistic equals the sum of squared
Pearson residuals.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Derive the <m>2\times2</m> odds-ratio formula</p>

<p>
<md>\widehat{OR}
=
\frac{ad}{bc}.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Show algebraically why the odds ratio approaches the relative risk when
both event probabilities are small.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Derive the approximate variance of</p>

<p>
<md>\log\widehat{OR}.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Construct a large-sample confidence interval for an odds ratio.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Derive the hypergeometric probability underlying Fisher's exact test.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why McNemar's test depends only on discordant pairs.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Show that the logistic function always satisfies</p>

<p>
<md>0&lt;p(x)&lt;1.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Derive</p>

<p>
<md>p
=
\frac{
e^\eta
}{
1+e^\eta
}</md>
</p>

<p>from</p>

<p>
<md>\log
\left(
\frac{p}{1-p}
\right)
=
\eta.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why</p>

<p>
<md>e^{\beta_j}</md>
</p>

<p>is an odds ratio in logistic regression.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why a logistic regression interaction changes the odds ratio for
one predictor across values of another predictor.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Compare Wald, score, and likelihood-ratio tests in logistic regression.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain the meaning of deviance.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why ordinary <m>R^2</m> does not directly extend to logistic
regression.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain the difference between discrimination and calibration.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain how complete separation affects logistic maximum likelihood.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Construct a baseline-category multinomial logit model for three outcome
categories.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain the proportional-odds assumption.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Show how independence in a two-way contingency table corresponds to a
